<p>
One of the things I like about <a href="https://www.italki.com/en/teacher/8899884">my regular tutor</a> is that he has a technical background (uses Linux and Neovim, experiments with AI), so I enjoy talking about projects and ideas with him. In my conversation classes, he does a good job of asking follow-up questions, suggesting things I could try, and correcting or filling in the occasional thing I'm trying to find a word for in French.
</p>

<p>
For fun, I decided to make a real-time display of this too: <a href="https://sachachua.com/blog/2026/10/using-emacs-to-visualize-speaking-and-target-words-phrases-in-real-ish-time/">Using Emacs to visualize speaking and target words/phrases in real-ish time</a>. I'm usually too focused on figuring out what to say in French to actually pay attention to that during the conversation itself, but maybe it'll be handy someday.
</p>

<p>
My next steps could include analyzing the other side of the
conversation to find out my average time to respond.
People usually start speaking <a href="https://journalofcognition.org/articles/10.5334/joc.268">within 200 milliseconds</a> of the end of
the other speaker's turn.
I take a lot longer because I'm still formulating my thoughts and
finding words, but it might be interesting to track my progress.</p>
